   "id": "10JzI88tAQAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2020-04-17T12:07:16Z",
   "subject": "Re: Modelling confounders in SEM",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I would include the green paths as well, otherwise your indirect effects are hard to interpret.  If you only estimate the yellow paths, then the &quot;b&quot; (and &quot;c&quot;) paths are interpreted as controlling for the confounders, but the &quot;a&quot; paths would not.  Seems strange to calculate indirect effects from mismatched direct effects.<div><br></div><div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792421871517000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0hVZUm4LyiHMSSsd1Uu--_\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div><div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
