     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Great thx!<div><br></div><div>So to visualize this for publication, what do you recommend? I used SEMpaths but that figure is unqorkable, see example: </div><div><br></div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/lavaan/attach/25050fb484645/Rplot01.png?part=0.1&amp;view=1\" alt=\"Rplot01.png\" width=\"320\" height=\"220\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\"></p></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><br>On Friday, 17 April 2020 14:07:16 UTC+2, Terrence Jorgensen  wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I would include the green paths as well, otherwise your indirect effects are hard to interpret.  If you only estimate the yellow paths, then the &quot;b&quot; (and &quot;c&quot;) paths are interpreted as controlling for the confounders, but the &quot;a&quot; paths would not.  Seems strange to calculate indirect effects from mismatched direct effects.<div><br></div><div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792421871517000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0hVZUm4LyiHMSSsd1Uu--_\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div><div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
